= Speech act — Technical =

== Austinian distinctions ==
A useful decomposition is:

<pre>
locutionary act
  └─ production of meaningful linguistic material

illocutionary act
  └─ socially/communicatively recognized force

perlocutionary effect
  └─ consequence produced in an audience
</pre>

The layers need not map one-to-one.

== Illocutionary force ==
Illocutionary force can be modeled using dimensions such as point, direction of fit, expressed psychological state, propositional content conditions, authority, and commitment.

Searlean taxonomies distinguish classes such as assertives, directives, commissives, expressives, and declarations.

These classes are theoretical models rather than exhaustive natural kinds.

== Felicity ==
Felicity conditions formalize requirements under which an act succeeds.

Institutional acts make this especially visible: authority, accepted procedure, role, and circumstance can be constitutive of success.

== Dynamic effects ==
Formal pragmatics can model acts as updates to conversational states:

<pre>
assert(p): propose/add p to commitments or common ground
ask(q):    raise an issue / partition alternatives
promise(a): add speaker commitment toward action a
</pre>

Specific update rules depend on the dialogue model.

== Indirect force ==
Indirect speech acts create a mapping problem between sentence semantics and communicative action. Analyses invoke convention, Gricean inference, relevance, constructional knowledge, probabilistic reasoning, or combinations.

